cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "SpectroscoPy: one way of handling spectra, whatever instrument they came from"
message: "If you use this software in work that you publish, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Sturgis
    given-names: James N.
    email: james.sturgis@univ-amu.fr
    affiliation: "Aix-Marseille Université, CNRS, LISM"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"   # TODO: add
repository-code: "https://github.com/jnsturgis/SpectroscoPy"
url: "https://jnsturgis.github.io/SpectroscoPy/"
abstract: >-
  SpectroscoPy is a Python library for spectra from several techniques --
  Raman, FTIR, UV-Vis and fluorescence -- read through one interface and
  handled with one data model. It reads the native binary formats instruments
  actually write, including Bruker OPUS and Galactic/Thermo SPC, alongside the
  usual text exports, and records the processing applied to a spectrum so that
  a published figure can be traced back to the raw files.
keywords:
  - spectroscopy
  - Raman
  - FTIR
  - ATR-FTIR
  - UV-Vis
  - fluorescence
  - JCAMP-DX
  - reproducibility
license: MPL-2.0
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-08-01"

# Notes for whoever updates this file.
#
# Update `version` and `date-released` on every tagged release. GitHub reads
# this file to render the "Cite this repository" button, so a stale version
# here is a wrong citation offered to every visitor.
#
# The package name on PyPI is not settled: `spectroscopy` is held by a dormant
# project and a transfer request is outstanding, with `pyspectroscopy` reserved
# as a fallback. Cite the repository URL above rather than a pip name until
# that resolves -- the URL is stable whichever way it goes. See
# SpectroscoPy_Codebase_Review.md section 16.
#
# When the JOSS paper is published, add a `preferred-citation:` block of type
# article pointing at it, with the DOI. Tools then cite the paper rather than
# the software, which is what you want; this block stays as the fallback for
# anyone citing a specific version.
